    "collected_at": "2026-11-20T18:13:42.244794+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/trucker-sentenced-in-7-2-million-cargo-theft-scheme-involving-legitimate-freight-contracts",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/trucker-sentenced-in-7-2-million-cargo-theft-scheme-involving-legitimate-freight-contracts",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-20T15:00:48+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-20T15:00:48+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-20T18:15:01.355004+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-20T18:15:01.355004+00:00",
    "body": "A California trucking company owner received 30 months in federal prison for participating in a $7.2 million cargo theft scheme. Criminals used legitimate transportation contracts to obtain shipments before diverting merchandise from warehouses across Southern California. The operation involved purchased trucking companies, stolen electronics and fraudulent driver identities. Federal investigators traced the activity from September 2021 through March 2025.\nThe Justice Department announced the sentence Tuesday, Nov. 17. U.S. District Judge Kenly Kiya Kato ordered Paramvir Singh, 30, to pay $2,210,055 in restitution. Singh pleaded guilty Aug. 13 to conspiracy to commit theft from interstate or foreign shipments. Prosecutors attributed approximately $7,210,056 in stolen merchandise to his criminal conduct. The restitution order is approximately $5 million lower than the attributed losses. Singh’s plea agreement reserved prosecutors’ right to appeal a restitution award below $7.2 million. The available court filings do not explain the discrepancy.\nCourt records describe how participants secured freight through brokers and online load boards . Drivers arrived at warehouses with tractors, trailers and paperwork identifying their assigned carriers. After collecting merchandise, conspirators either withheld portions of shipments or diverted entire loads. The operation targeted facilities in Walnut, Fontana, Moreno Valley and Chino, California.\nOn June 23, 2023, Singh and two co-conspirators transported a trailer containing $5 million in stolen cargo. Federal court records identify a truck yard in San Bernardino, California, as the destination. Investigators also uncovered unauthorized identity use during transportation-related activities. A September 2025 indictment detailed the methods behind the multiyear conspiracy.\nTrucking companies provided access to freight\nSingh owned Janjua Transport, which prosecutors connected to several stolen shipments. Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration records identify the company under USDOT 3620976 and MC 1236841. The Bakersfield, California, carrier listed one truck and one driver. Regulators granted its operating authority June 11, 2021, before revoking it June 14, 2022. The case illustrates how criminals can exploit legitimate carrier authorities to secure freight before diverting shipments.\nFederal court filings identify AP Freight Truckline among businesses associated with Singh’s activities. A carrier profile for AP Freight Trucklines Inc. identifies Paramvir Singh as an officer. The court filings use a slightly different company name. The carrier’s registration lists USDOT 3932312 and MC 1457821. Carrier intelligence platform FreightCheck identified shared contact information between the companies. Both listed the same Bakersfield address and email address. Each carrier also reported one truck and one driver. Regulators granted AP Freight Trucklines operating authority Sept. 19, 2022, before revoking it Dec. 27, 2022.\nFMCSA records show regulators reinstated AP Freight Trucklines’ authority Feb. 28, 2023. They revoked it again July 26, 2023. Neither company currently holds active interstate operating authority or maintains primary liability insurance on file. According to the government’s sentencing memorandum, Singh reported annual earnings of $4.1 million from Janjua Transport. He also reported $1.5 million annually from the business identified as AP Freight Truckline in court filings. Prosecutors referenced those figures while examining his financial circumstances.\nOn March 12, 2022, Mandeep Singh, Sandeep Singh and Gurnaik Singh Chauhan collected merchandise from a Fontana warehouse. The indictment identifies all three as purporting to work for Janjua Transport. Paramvir Singh joined them in transferring the shipment into separate vehicles at a nearby storage facility. Prosecutors valued the stolen cargo at $185,734.40.\nAdditional incidents occurred in Moreno Valley during September and October 2022. Destinations included three California cities: Lebec, Riverside and Rialto. Another shipment was headed to St. George, Utah. None reached their intended delivery locations, according to investigators. Individual losses ranged from approximately $129,000 to more than $421,000.\nThe operation also involved purchasing established motor carriers . On Sept. 29, 2023, Singh acquired Obey Eagle Inc. and an associated tractor. Court records document Zelle payments between conspirators and the previous owner. Investigators later connected that vehicle to an unsuccessful freight pickup attempt in Mira Loma, California.\nFederal filings describe another incident involving a transportation marketplace account. On March 24, 2025, Singh attempted to register with Flexport using another individual’s identification. The indictment identifies Convoy as the company’s associated load board. Prosecutors included the attempted registration among the conspiracy’s final documented activities.\nFour defendants await trial\nThe original indictment named seven defendants. Charges included wire fraud, interstate cargo theft, identity theft and money laundering. Singh’s guilty plea resolved his conspiracy charge involving stolen commercial shipments. Federal prosecutors identified four additional individuals awaiting trial in March 2027. Their charges remain pending, and the defendants retain the presumption of innocence.\nThose defendants include Sandeep Singh, 32, of San Bernardino, and Gurnaik Singh Chauhan, 41, of Fontana. Ranjodh Singh, 39, of Bakersfield, also faces trial alongside Elgar Rolando Hernández, 28, of Fontana. The original filing additionally named Mandeep Singh and Harpreet Singh. Tuesday’s announcement did not provide their current case dispositions.\nThe FBI’s Inland Violent Crime Suppression Task Force investigated the conspiracy. IRS Criminal Investigation and the California Highway Patrol’s Cargo Theft Interdiction Program also participated. Sheriff’s departments in San Bernardino and Riverside counties contributed additional resources. Police agencies in Fontana and San Bernardino also assisted. Federal prosecutors continue pursuing the remaining defendants through the Central District of California.\nWhy it matters\nCriminals exploited legitimate freight transactions to steal millions in cargo without traditional hijackings. Their ability to secure loads and redirect shipments demonstrates how easily fraud can enter routine transportation operations. The case highlights the financial exposure brokers, carriers and shippers face when criminals manipulate established freight processes.\nCFCO\nThe Certified Fraud Compliance Officer (CFCO) program addresses the risks criminals exploit when obtaining freight through legitimate transportation channels. The training emphasizes identifying suspicious booking activity, verifying pickup authorization and recognizing discrepancies before cargo changes hands. A legitimate carrier profile does not eliminate fraud risk when criminals manipulate the people and processes behind a shipment.\nClick here for more articles on cargo theft and freight fraud by Phil Brink.\n14 charged in $22.5 million cargo theft ring that stopped moving freight trains – FreightWaves\nABF Freight workers uncover $1.5M drug shipment headed to Ohio – FreightWaves\nSuspect in $100M Brink’s jewelry theft agrees to plead guilty in related cargo robbery case – FreightWaves",
